:1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"Correct on the central point: a new series added by a Rule 485(a) post-effective amendment normally becomes effective on the 75th day after filing. The answer also rightly treats effectiveness as a registration step, not a launch guarantee. It lists real follow-on steps: exchange listing, AP agreements, seed capital, service providers, NSCC/DTC readiness, and staff comments. It correctly says a Rule 6c-11 ETF needs no exemptive order.\\n\\nHowever, the requested distinction for a new trust is materially wrong. The answer says a brand-new trust files under 'Section 8(a) and Rule 485(a)' and 'typically also faces the 75-day period.' Rule 485 governs post-effective amendments, so it does not cover a new trust's initial registration. The grader notes say not to present 75 days as the rule for a new trust. That section is also cut off mid-sentence.\\n\\nOmissions: it does not mention the 60-day period for other material amendments. It does not mention designated later effective dates (at most 95 days for a new-series amendment), though it does reference delaying amendments and earlier action.\"}","ttft_ms":2556,"type":"result","duration_ms":5658,"uuid":"463d363d-0710-45e4-ba43-9a56ea10d745","ttft_stream_ms":671,"time_to_request_ms":84,"first_content_frame_ms":671,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
